Specification UserInterface

Description The windows the program is worked in: a list of accounts, the
\ register of the account selected in it, and an optional second pane
\ that shows either another account or an import waiting to be reviewed.
Details
\ The shape is three regions. On the left, every account, grouped by the
\ kind of thing it is. On the right, the register of whichever account is
\ selected. The right may be split in two, so that two accounts can be
\ read at once -- or so that an import can be looked over beside the
\ register it is about to change.
\
\ What is specified here is what the windows show and what selecting
\ things does. Pixels and fonts are not, and neither is the width a
\ column starts at: those are not worth agreeing in a table, and
\ changing them should not fail a test.
\
\ Column widths used to be on that list and are not any more. What a
\ column is wide by default is still nobody's business here, but a width
\ a person set by dragging is: it has to outlast the register it was set
\ in and outlast the session, and that is behaviour rather than
\ appearance. See the width of a column, at the end of this file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The account list
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType AccountGroup
Description The headings the account list is divided into. One per kind of real
\ account, and the categories are not in the list at all: they are
\ reached through a report, not browsed.
Details The words Asset and Liability are not headings here. Which side of a
\ balance sheet an account is on is a different question, asked by the
\ balance sheet and nowhere else; see the balance-side rule in CoreTypes.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value            | Notes                                      |
| Banking          | Checking, savings and cash                 |
| Credit           | What is owed on a card or a line of credit |
| Investments      | Holdings and their cash                    |
| LoanAndDebt      | A mortgage or a loan                       |
| PropertyAndAsset | A house, a car, anything else owned        |
| Business         | What is owed to and by the business        |
| Transfer         | Money on its way between two accounts      |

BusinessRule Which group an account is listed under and how the heading reads
Description Taken from the account type, so nothing has to be filed by hand.
Details The nominal types have no group: Income, Expense and Equity are
\ categories and the account list does not show them. Their heading is
\ given here as an empty cell rather than a word, because there is none.
\
\ The heading on the page is given beside the name because two of them
\ carry an ampersand and a space, and a name in this specification is an
\ identifier.
Examples: GroupOfType
| AccountType        | AccountGroup     | Heading          | Listed |
| Checking           | Banking          | Banking          | true   |
| Savings            | Banking          | Banking          | true   |
| Cash               | Banking          | Banking          | true   |
| CreditCard         | Credit           | Credit           | true   |
| LineOfCredit       | Credit           | Credit           | true   |
| Brokerage          | Investments      | Investments      | true   |
| IRA                | Investments      | Investments      | true   |
| HSA                | Investments      | Investments      | true   |
| Retirement401k     | Investments      | Investments      | true   |
| Mortgage           | LoanAndDebt      | Loan~&~Debt      | true   |
| AutoLoan           | LoanAndDebt      | Loan~&~Debt      | true   |
| PersonalLoan       | LoanAndDebt      | Loan~&~Debt      | true   |
| OtherDebt          | LoanAndDebt      | Loan~&~Debt      | true   |
| RealEstate         | PropertyAndAsset | Property~&~Asset | true   |
| Vehicle            | PropertyAndAsset | Property~&~Asset | true   |
| OtherAsset         | PropertyAndAsset | Property~&~Asset | true   |
| AccountsReceivable | Business         | Business         | true   |
| AccountsPayable    | Business         | Business         | true   |
| TransferIn         | Transfer         | Transfer         | true   |
| TransferOut        | Transfer         | Transfer         | true   |
| Income             | Banking          |                  | false  |
| Expense            | Banking          |                  | false  |
| Equity             | Banking          |                  | false  |
# A tilde in a cell is a space, so the heading of the fourth group reads
# "Loan & Debt". The AccountGroup column has to hold something for a category
# and holds Banking, which Listed being false is what makes harmless.

Attributes GroupOfType
| Attribute    | Type         | Default  |
| AccountType  | AccountType  | Checking |
| AccountGroup | AccountGroup | Banking  |
| Heading      | Text         | none     |
| Listed       | Boolean      | true     |

Attributes AccountListRow
Description One line of the account list.
Details A group heading carries the total of the accounts under it and names no
\ account; an account names no group. The groups are listed in the order
\ they are declared, and a group with nothing in it is left out rather
\ than shown empty.
\
\ Indent is the depth on the page: a group heading is 0, an account under
\ it is 1, and a child of that account is 2.
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Group     | Text        | none    |
| Account   | AccountPath | none    |
| Name      | Text        | none    |
| Indent    | Integer     | 0       |
| Balance   | Money       | 0.00    |
| IsHeading | Boolean     | false   |

Background A book with a few accounts
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Assets:Savings         | Checking   | false       |
| Assets:Wallet          | Cash       | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense    | false       |
| Income:Salary          | Income     | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-01-05 | Acme Payroll  | Income:Salary      | 3000.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00  |
| T3  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-01-20 | Amazon        | Expenses:Groceries | -89.99  |
| T4  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-01-25 | Transfer      | Assets:Savings     | -500.00 |

Scenario The account list groups the real accounts and totals each group
Description Each balance is the display balance, so a card reads as what is
\ owed. A group heading carries the total of what is under it.
\ The categories are absent: Expenses:Groceries and Income:Salary
\ have transactions and are not accounts to browse.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account list shown
Then account list rows are : AccountListRow
| Group   | Account          | Name     | Indent | Balance | IsHeading |
| Banking | none             | none     | 0      | 2955.00 | true      |
| none    | Assets:Checking  | Checking | 1      | 2455.00 | false     |
| none    | Assets:Savings   | Savings  | 1      | 500.00  | false     |
| none    | Assets:Wallet    | Wallet   | 1      | 0.00    | false     |
| Credit  | none             | none     | 0      | 89.99   | true      |
| none    | Liabilities:Visa | Visa     | 1      | 89.99   | false     |

Scenario Every group that has an account in it is a heading
Description One heading per kind of account, in the order the groups are
\ declared, and nothing above them. A card is under Credit and a
\ mortgage under Loan & Debt; neither the word Asset nor the word
\ Liability is on the page, because the list is for reaching an
\ account and the balance sheet is where the two sides are read.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                 | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking      | Checking   | false       |
| Assets:Savings       | Savings    | false       |
| Assets:Brokerage     | Brokerage  | false       |
| Assets:House         | RealEstate | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa     | CreditCard | false       |
| Liabilities:Mortgage | Mortgage   | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount        | Date       | Payee | Destination | Amount     |
| T1  | Assets:Checking      | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 2455.00    |
| T2  | Assets:Savings       | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 500.00     |
| T3  | Assets:Brokerage     | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 9000.00    |
| T4  | Assets:House         | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 200000.00  |
| T5  | Liabilities:Visa     | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | -89.99     |
| T6  | Liabilities:Mortgage | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | -150000.00 |
When account list shown
Then account list rows are : AccountListRow
| Group            | Account              | Name      | Indent | Balance   | IsHeading |
| Banking          | none                 | none      | 0      | 2955.00   | true      |
| none             | Assets:Checking      | Checking  | 1      | 2455.00   | false     |
| none             | Assets:Savings       | Savings   | 1      | 500.00    | false     |
| Credit           | none                 | none      | 0      | 89.99     | true      |
| none             | Liabilities:Visa     | Visa      | 1      | 89.99     | false     |
| Investments      | none                 | none      | 0      | 9000.00   | true      |
| none             | Assets:Brokerage     | Brokerage | 1      | 9000.00   | false     |
| LoanAndDebt      | none                 | none      | 0      | 150000.00 | true      |
| none             | Liabilities:Mortgage | Mortgage  | 1      | 150000.00 | false     |
| PropertyAndAsset | none                 | none      | 0      | 200000.00 | true      |
| none             | Assets:House         | House     | 1      | 200000.00 | false     |
# Every figure is the display balance, so the card and the mortgage read as
# what is owed. Business and Transfer have nothing in them and are left out.
# The Group cell holds the group's name; what the heading reads on the page is
# the Heading column of the rule above, so this list shows Loan & Debt and
# Property & Asset.

Scenario A group with no accounts is not shown
Description There is nothing under Investments, Assets or Liabilities in this
\ book, so those headings are left out rather than shown empty.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account list shown
Then account list groups shown are : GroupShown
| Group   |
| Banking |
| Credit  |

Attributes GroupShown
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Group     | Text |         |

Scenario A hidden account is left out until it is asked for
Description Hiding an account is how it is got out of the way without losing its
\ history, so the list follows that. The total of the group follows
\ the list, so hiding an account changes what the heading says.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
| Assets:Old      | Checking | false       | true   |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee | Destination | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 100.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Old      | 2026-01-06 | Open  | none        | 25.00  |
When account list shown
Then account list rows are : AccountListRow
| Group   | Account         | Name     | Indent | Balance | IsHeading |
| Banking | none            | none     | 0      | 100.00  | true      |
| none    | Assets:Checking | Checking | 1      | 100.00  | false     |
When hidden accounts shown
Then account list rows are : AccountListRow
| Group   | Account         | Name     | Indent | Balance | IsHeading |
| Banking | none            | none     | 0      | 125.00  | true      |
| none    | Assets:Checking | Checking | 1      | 100.00  | false     |
| none    | Assets:Old      | Old      | 1      | 25.00   | false     |

Scenario A placeholder is a line in the list with its subtree beneath it
Description A placeholder takes no transactions of its own, so its balance is the
\ roll-up. It is shown because it is how a deep tree is navigated.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Brokerage        | Brokerage  | true  |
| Assets:Brokerage:Cash   | Brokerage  | false |
| Assets:Brokerage:Fund   | Brokerage  | false |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount         | Date       | Payee | Destination | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Brokerage:Cash | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 2500.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Brokerage:Fund | 2026-01-06 | Open  | none        | 7500.00 |
When account list shown
Then account list rows are : AccountListRow
| Group       | Account               | Name      | Indent | Balance  | IsHeading |
| Investments | none                  | none      | 0      | 10000.00 | true      |
| none        | Assets:Brokerage      | Brokerage | 1      | 10000.00 | false     |
| none        | Assets:Brokerage:Cash | Cash      | 2      | 2500.00  | false     |
| none        | Assets:Brokerage:Fund | Fund      | 2      | 7500.00  | false     |

Scenario A legacy root is not a line of its own
Description Assets and Liabilities were the only heading an account had before
\ groups existed, and adding an account still creates one as an
\ ordinary placeholder above it. Now that the group heading says the
\ same thing, that placeholder would say it twice -- Banking, then
\ Assets, then Checking -- so it has no line of its own. Its balance
\ still counts in the group heading above it, because the money is
\ still there; only the redundant line is gone.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets          | Checking | true        |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Savings  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee | Destination | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 2455.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Savings  | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 500.00  |
When account list shown
Then account list rows are : AccountListRow
| Group   | Account         | Name     | Indent | Balance | IsHeading |
| Banking | none            | none     | 0      | 2955.00 | true      |
| none    | Assets:Checking | Checking | 1      | 2455.00 | false     |
| none    | Assets:Savings  | Savings  | 1      | 500.00  | false     |
# Checking and Savings are one level in from the Banking heading, not two:
# Assets is walked past rather than counted. A meaningful placeholder such as
# Brokerage, in the scenario above, still gets its own line -- it says
# something a heading does not.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Selecting an account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes PaneState
Description What one pane of the right-hand side is showing.
| Attribute | Type        | Default  |
| Pane      | Integer     | 1        |
| Showing   | PaneContent | Register |
| Account   | AccountPath | none     |
| Active    | Boolean     | false    |

DataType PaneContent
Description What a pane can show.
Details An import under review and a find's results used to be a second kind
\ of pane, shown beside this one. They are a tab of the bottom panel
\ now, so a pane is always exactly one per open tab; see the bottom-panel
\ section below.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value    | Notes                                                  |
| Register | The transactions of one account                        |
| Report   | A report, with its own period and its own settings     |
| Empty    | Nothing selected yet                                   |

Scenario Selecting an account shows its register
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then panes are : PaneState
| Pane | Showing  | Account         | Active |
| 1    | Register | Assets:Checking | true   |
And register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Balance |
| T1  | 2026-01-05 | Acme Payroll  | 3000.00 |
| T2  | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | 2955.00 |
| T4  | 2026-01-25 | Transfer      | 2455.00 |

Scenario Selecting a placeholder shows everything beneath it
Description A placeholder has no transactions of its own, so showing an empty
\ register would be useless. It shows the subtree, which is what a
\ reader of a brokerage account wants: the cash and the holdings
\ together.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                  | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Brokerage      | Brokerage  | true        |
| Assets:Brokerage:Cash | Brokerage  | false       |
| Assets:Brokerage:Fund | Brokerage  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount         | Date       | Payee | Destination | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Brokerage:Cash | 2026-01-05 | Open  | none        | 2500.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Brokerage:Fund | 2026-01-06 | Open  | none        | 7500.00 |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Assets:Brokerage |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Date       | Balance  |
| T1  | 2026-01-05 | 2500.00  |
| T2  | 2026-01-06 | 10000.00 |

Scenario The other side of a transfer is named in the category column
Description A transfer appears in both registers, and in each of them the
\ category column names the other account. It is one transaction seen
\ twice, not two.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Savings  |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Date       | Category   | Payment | Deposit |
| T4  | 2026-01-25 | [Checking] | 0.00    | 500.00  |
# Shown in brackets with the root segment dropped, the way Quicken shows one:
# the group heading on the account list already says what kind of account
# this is. See register_lines.h.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The right-hand side is tabs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A bar of tabs, each one a register or a report, and one of them is
\ the active tab.
Details Several things stay open at once because the work moves between them. A
\ card statement is reconciled against the card; the payment that
\ settles it is in the current account; whether it has cleared is
\ answered by looking at both. Two reports over two periods answer
\ whether a month was unusual. Closing one to open the other and back
\ again is the work a tab bar does away with.
\
\ The active tab is where a transaction typed on the blank line goes,
\ without being asked about: the question "into which account" is
\ answered by what is in front of you. An import is the one thing that
\ does ask, because the file may not be the file you think it is and a
\ statement in the wrong account is expensive to undo. The active tab is
\ what it proposes when the file gives it nothing to go on; see where an
\ import is going is said before it goes there, below.
\
\ One tab per account. Selecting an account already open returns to its
\ tab rather than opening a second: two tabs on one register would show
\ the same lines and leave which to believe an open question.
\
\ Reports are the other way round. Asking for a report opens another tab
\ every time, because the reason to have two is to read two periods side
\ by side, and a report that replaced the one already open could not do
\ that.
\
\ The import review is not a tab. It is the second pane, beside the
\ register it is about to change, and only while the import is waiting;
\ see the section below. A tab is something to come back to, and the
\ second pane is a comparison that lasts as long as the comparison does.
\
\ Pane 1 is the tab area and its account is the active tab's, which is
\ why the scenarios here can speak of panes and of tabs at once without
\ the two disagreeing.

DataType TabKind
Description What one tab holds.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value    | Notes                                              |
| Register | The transactions of one account                     |
| Report   | A report, with its own period and its own settings  |

Attributes OpenTab
Description One tab of the tab bar, in the order the tabs are shown.
Details A register tab is named by the account's own name and not its path: a
\ tab is narrow, and the path is on the account list where there is room
\ for it. A report tab is named by the report; a second tab of the same
\ report has a 2 after it, and so on, because the period is shown on the
\ controls inside the tab where there is room to read it.
| Attribute | Type        | Default  |
| Position  | Integer     | 1        |
| Kind      | TabKind     | Register |
| Account   | AccountPath | none     |
| Name      | Text        | none     |
| Active    | Boolean     | false    |

Attributes TabSelect
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Position  | Integer | 1       |

Scenario Selecting an account opens its register in a tab
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind     | Account         | Name     | Active |
| 1        | Register | Assets:Checking | Checking | true   |
And panes are : PaneState
| Pane | Showing  | Account         | Active |
| 1    | Register | Assets:Checking | true   |

Scenario Selecting a second account leaves the first one open
Description This is the whole point. The card and the current account are both
\ open and the eye moves between them.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And account selected : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind     | Account          | Name     | Active |
| 1        | Register | Assets:Checking  | Checking | false  |
| 2        | Register | Liabilities:Visa | Visa     | true   |
And panes are : PaneState
| Pane | Showing  | Account          | Active |
| 1    | Register | Liabilities:Visa | true   |
# A new tab goes at the end and becomes the active one, because selecting an
# account is asking to read it.

Scenario Selecting an account that is already open returns to its tab
Description Not a second tab on the same register. Two tabs showing the same
\ lines would leave which of them to believe an open question, and
\ nothing would ever answer it.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And account selected : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
And account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind     | Account          | Name     | Active |
| 1        | Register | Assets:Checking  | Checking | true   |
| 2        | Register | Liabilities:Visa | Visa     | false  |
# Two tabs, not three, and the first is active again. The order does not change
# either: a tab stays where it was put, or the bar would rearrange itself under
# the hand that is using it.

Scenario Asking for a report opens it in a tab of its own
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category   |
| From     | 2026-01-01 |
| To       | 2026-12-31 |
| Interval | Whole      |
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind     | Account         | Name                 | Active |
| 1        | Register | Assets:Checking | Checking             | false  |
| 2        | Report   | none            | Spending by category | true   |

Scenario Asking for the same report again opens a second tab
Description Two periods side by side is the reason to have two, so a report
\ does not replace the one already open.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category   |
| From     | 2026-01-01 |
| To       | 2026-12-31 |
| Interval | Whole      |
And report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category   |
| From     | 2025-01-01 |
| To       | 2025-12-31 |
| Interval | Whole      |
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind   | Account | Name                   | Active |
| 1        | Report | none    | Spending by category   | false  |
| 2        | Report | none    | Spending by category 2 | true   |
# The second carries a 2 so the two can be told apart on a narrow tab. Which
# period each one is over is on the controls inside it, where there is room.

Scenario Clicking an open tab makes it active, whatever kind it is
Description A tab bar is clicked by position, not by what is on the tab, so
\ this is the step a click actually produces -- unlike selecting an
\ account, which is also how a tab gets opened in the first place.
\ Without it, clicking back to a report that lost focus has nothing
\ to tell the workspace, and the next redraw puts the register back.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category   |
| From     | 2026-01-01 |
| To       | 2026-12-31 |
| Interval | Whole      |
And tab selected : TabSelect Vertical
| Position | 1 |
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind     | Account         | Name                  | Active |
| 1        | Register | Assets:Checking | Checking              | true   |
| 2        | Report   | none            | Spending by category  | false  |
When tab selected : TabSelect Vertical
| Position | 2 |
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind     | Account         | Name                  | Active |
| 1        | Register | Assets:Checking | Checking              | false  |
| 2        | Report   | none            | Spending by category  | true   |

