| Pane | Showing | Account | Active |
| 1    | Empty   | none    | true   |
# The empty tab table says there are none. The converter does not generate a
# step for a table with no rows yet -- finding 6 -- so for now that line is
# documentation and the pane assertion is what carries the scenario.

Scenario Opening another book leaves none of the tabs of the one before
Description A tab is a register of an account, and the accounts of the book now
\ open are not the accounts of the one that was -- a tab left over
\ from the book before names an account this one may not have, and a
\ report tab left over holds totals of transactions that are not in
\ it. So every tab goes when the book goes, whatever kind it is, and
\ the window is as it is when it first opens a book.
Details Every other change hands the book over and leaves the tabs alone: a
\ transaction entered, a category changed, an import accepted. They are
\ changes to the book that is open, and what is being read is still
\ worth reading. Another book is the one case where it is not.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
And transactions are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category   |
| From     | 2026-01-01 |
| To       | 2026-12-31 |
| Interval | Whole      |
And another book is opened
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind | Account | Name | Active |
And panes are : PaneState
| Pane | Showing | Account | Active |
| 1    | Empty   | none    | true   |
# A register tab and a report tab, so that both kinds have to go. The empty
# tab table is documentation again, and the pane is what carries it: nothing
# is showing, because there is no tab left to show.

