# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where an import is going is said before it goes there
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A file chosen to import is not read into whichever register happens
\ to be in front. The window says which account it is about to put the
\ statement in, and waits for that to be confirmed.
Details The account comes from the file wherever the file knows it. A QFX names
\ the institution and the account number it was downloaded for, and the
\ account of this book that carries that number is the answer, so what
\ is in front is beside the point. A CSV names nothing, so the name of
\ the file is all there is to read, and it usually carries the account or
\ its number because the bank wrote it. Neither is certain, which is why
\ it is confirmed rather than acted on.
\
\ Where the file stated them, the bank id and the account number are
\ shown in the dialog alongside the account. They are the only part of
\ it that did not come from this program, and they are what a person
\ compares against the statement in their hand.
\
\ When neither the file nor its name says anything, the register in front
\ is proposed, because that is nearly always what is meant -- it is the
\ statement being reconciled. What is new is that it is said before the
\ file is read rather than discovered after.
\
\ A statement in the wrong account is expensive to undo. Every row of it
\ has to be found and deleted; worse, a row the import called a
\ duplicate of something in the wrong account was never brought in at
\ all, so the account it belonged in is short and nothing says so. One
\ dialog against that is cheap.
\
\ The dialog is in front of the file, not after it: nothing is read, no
\ tab is opened and the bottom panel stays shut until it is confirmed.
\
\ Confirming it does two things at once. The register of the account it
\ settled becomes the active tab -- opened if it was not open, brought
\ forward if it was -- and the rows of the file open in the panel
\ beneath that register. So the statement is read against the lines it
\ is about to join, which is the whole reason the review is a panel
\ under a register rather than a dialog of its own.

DataType ImportFileFormat
Description What kind of file was chosen, and what that kind of file can say
\ about the account it is for.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value | Notes                                                               |
| Qfx   | OFX or QFX. Names its institution and account number, so the account is in the file |
| Qif   | Names an account in an !Account block when it has one, and nothing when it has not |
| Csv   | Says nothing about itself; the name of the file is all there is      |

Attributes ImportFileChosen
Description The file the user picked, and what the import made of what is inside
\ it before any of its rows were read.
Details AccountInFile is the account of this book the file turned out to be
\ for -- for a QFX, the account that carries the number the download
\ was made for; see which account a download is for in ImportOfx. It is
\ blank when the file names nothing, and blank when it names a number no
\ account in this book carries, because an account that is not in the
\ book is not an answer. How it is arrived at is the import's own
\ business and is specified there; here it is given.
\
\ BankId and AccountNumber are what the file said about itself, whether
\ or not an account was found for them. They are blank for a CSV and for
\ a QIF, which say nothing of the kind, and they are not blank for a QFX
\ whose number is unknown -- that is the case where they matter most,
\ because they are the whole of what the user has to go on when asked
\ which account the download belongs to.
| Attribute       | Type             | Default |
| FileName        | Text             |         |
| Format          | ImportFileFormat | Csv     |
| AccountInFile   | AccountPath      | none    |
| BankId          | Text             | none    |
| AccountNumber   | Text             | none    |
| OnlineBalance   | Text             | none    |
| OnlineBalanceAt | Text             | none    |

DataType ImportTargetSource
Description Where the account in the confirmation came from. It is shown, because
\ "we read this out of the file" and "this is the tab you were on" are
\ worth different amounts of trust.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value       | Notes                                                         |
| File        | The file named it and the book has it                         |
| FileName    | The name of the file fitted one account and only one          |
| ActiveTab   | Neither did, so the register in front                         |
| NoneOffered | Nothing to propose. The dialog asks with no account in it      |

Attributes ImportConfirmation
Description The dialog, as it stands. Shown is false before a file is chosen and
\ after the dialog is answered either way.
Details BankId and AccountNumber are shown because they are the only things in
\ the dialog that come from the bank rather than from this program, and
\ they are what settles the question when the proposed account is wrong.
\ A person who sees the number on their statement knows at once whether
\ the right register is being offered; a file name and an account path
\ are both this program repeating itself back.
\
\ They are shown as the file wrote them, not reformatted, so that they
\ can be read against a statement. A download that states neither
\ leaves both lines out of the dialog rather than showing an empty one.
| Attribute       | Type               | Default     |
| Shown           | Boolean            | true        |
| FileName        | Text               | none        |
| Account         | AccountPath        | none        |
| Source          | ImportTargetSource | NoneOffered |
| BankId          | Text               | none        |
| AccountNumber   | Text               | none        |
| OnlineBalance   | Text               | none        |
| OnlineBalanceAt | Text               | none        |

BusinessRule Which account the import proposes
Description The file first, its name second, the register in front last. Each
\ one is only consulted because the one before it had nothing to say.
Details A candidate is an account a register can be opened on: not a
\ placeholder, not a category, and not a hidden account. FromName is the
\ one candidate the name of the file fits -- blank if none fits, and
\ blank if two do, because a name that fits two accounts has not named
\ either of them.
\
\ The last row is the one case with no answer at all: a file that says
\ nothing, read while a report is in front, so there is no register to
\ fall back on. The dialog is shown anyway, with nothing in the account
\ line, and the account is named there. A person holding a statement
\ knows which account it is for; this program being unable to work it
\ out is no reason to refuse to read the file. Refusing it was the
\ earlier rule here, and what it amounted to was making a statement
\ unreadable because no register happened to be open.
Examples: ImportTargetProposal
| Format | AccountInFile    | FromName        | ActiveTab        | Proposed         | Source      |
| Qfx    | Liabilities:Visa | none            | Assets:Checking  | Liabilities:Visa | File        |
| Qfx    | Liabilities:Visa | Assets:Checking | Assets:Checking  | Liabilities:Visa | File        |
| Qfx    | Liabilities:Visa | none            | none             | Liabilities:Visa | File        |
| Qfx    | none             | none            | Assets:Checking  | Assets:Checking  | ActiveTab   |
| Qif    | Assets:Savings   | none            | Assets:Checking  | Assets:Savings   | File        |
| Csv    | none             | Assets:Checking | Liabilities:Visa | Assets:Checking  | FileName    |
| Csv    | none             | none            | Assets:Checking  | Assets:Checking  | ActiveTab   |
| Csv    | none             | none            | none             | none             | NoneOffered |
# The second row is the one that says which way round it is: the name of the
# file disagreed with the inside of the file, and the inside won.

Attributes ImportTargetProposal
| Attribute     | Type               | Default     |
| Format        | ImportFileFormat   | Csv         |
| AccountInFile | AccountPath        | none        |
| FromName      | AccountPath        | none        |
| ActiveTab     | AccountPath        | none        |
| Proposed      | AccountPath        | none        |
| Source        | ImportTargetSource | NoneOffered |

Description The dialog also asks what the bank says the balance is, filled in
\ for a file that states one and empty for a file that does not.
Details A download that states LEDGERBAL is checked against the book, and the
\ disagreement, when there is one, is the most useful thing an import
\ ever says. A CSV states nothing of the kind, so that check has
\ simply never been made on one -- and a CSV is what several banks
\ here will give. The number is on the website the file was downloaded
\ from, in front of the person doing the downloading, and a box to
\ type it into is the whole of what was missing.
\
\ So the line is in the dialog for every format. A QFX arrives with it
\ filled in, and it can be corrected; a CSV or a QIF arrives with it
\ empty, and typing it is optional. What is typed is used for exactly
\ what the file's own figure is used for: the check at the end of the
\ import, and the balance written on the account afterwards. See the
\ two balances at the top of the register in TransactionRegister, and
\ the balance a download states in ImportOfx.
\
\ Left empty, nothing happens -- no check is made and the account's
\ balance from the bank, if it has one, is left as it was. An import
\ nobody has a balance for is the ordinary case for twenty years of
\ history and is not an error.
\
\ A date goes with it, defaulting to the latest date in the file,
\ because a balance without a date cannot be compared with anything.
\ For a QFX it is the date LEDGERBAL states.

Attributes ImportConfirmationAnswer
Description The dialog, answered. OnlineBalance empty is the answer that there
\ is no figure, which is different from zero.
| Attribute       | Type        | Default |
| Account         | AccountPath |         |
| OnlineBalance   | Text        | none    |
| OnlineBalanceAt | Text        | none    |

