# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Asking whether two rows are one transfer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A row being reviewed can be the other half of a transaction the book
\ already has. The amount and the date are all there is to say so, and
\ that is not enough to act on, so the question is put while the review
\ is still open.
Details Which rows pair with which is in ImportOfx, under the rule about a half
\ already in the book. This section is only what the window does with
\ one: a question per candidate, naming both halves -- each one's
\ account, date and payee, as its own file wrote it -- and the gap in
\ days, which is the thing a person most needs in order to disagree.
\
\ Nothing is written while a question is open, and an unanswered question
\ is not a refusal. Leaving one alone says the two are separate, which is
\ the safe reading, and the review can be accepted with questions still
\ unanswered. What cannot happen is a pairing being made without having
\ been answered.
\
\ Not asking at all was the earlier rule, and the cost of it was every
\ card payment that stayed two transactions and counted twice in the
\ spending. Asking a question a person can answer in a second is a much
\ smaller cost than that.

# The dialog's own wording is in the app, where a spec test cannot read it,
# and the third answer does not remember anything yet.
BusinessRule What the transfer question says
Description Enough of both halves to tell two separate payments of the same
\ amount from one transfer, and no more than that.
Details The heading is a question rather than a statement because the program
\ does not know the answer. Everything under it is quoted from a file or
\ from the book, so that none of it is this program's own reasoning
\ repeated back.
Examples: TransferQuestionLine
| Line      | Reads                                                          |
| Heading   | Are these the same payment                                     |
| FirstHalf | The account, date and payee of the half already in the book     |
| OtherHalf | The account, date and payee of the row being reviewed           |
| Gap       | How many days apart the two dates are                           |
| Answers   | Yes; No; Yes, and do not ask again for these two                |

Attributes TransferQuestionLine
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Line      | Text |         |
| Reads     | Text |         |

Attributes TransferQuestion
Description The question as it stands. Shown is false when there is nothing to
\ ask, and after the last question has been answered.
| Attribute   | Type        | Default    |
| Shown       | Boolean     | true       |
| FromAccount | AccountPath | none       |
| FromDate    | Date        | 2026-01-01 |
| FromPayee   | Text        | none       |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath | none       |
| ToDate      | Date        | 2026-01-01 |
| ToPayee     | Text        | none       |
| Amount      | Money       | 0.00       |
| DaysApart   | Integer     | 0          |

# YesAndRemember is not told from Yes yet.
DataType TransferAnswer
Description What the person said to one question.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value          | Notes                                                      |
| Yes            | One transfer; this once                                    |
| No             | Two separate payments, and do not ask again about this row |
| YesAndRemember | One transfer, and pair these two payees without asking     |

Attributes TransferAnswerGiven
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Answer    | TransferAnswer | Yes     |

Scenario A reviewed row that pairs with a transaction already here is asked about
Description The card file is read a month after the checking file. The payment
\ is already in the book as a row of the checking account with a
\ category on it, and the card's own row of it is being reviewed now.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense    | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee        | Destination            | Amount   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-03-02 | VISA PAYMENT | Expenses:Uncategorized | -3348.07 |
And the file holds : ImportReviewRow
| Line | Date       | Payee            | Category               | Amount  |
| 1    | 2026-03-03 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Expenses:Uncategorized | 3348.07 |
When import confirmed into : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then transfer question is : TransferQuestion Vertical
| Shown       | true             |
| FromAccount | Assets:Checking  |
| FromDate    | 2026-03-02       |
| FromPayee   | VISA PAYMENT     |
| ToAccount   | Liabilities:Visa |
| ToDate      | 2026-03-03       |
| ToPayee     | PAYMENT RECEIVED |
| Amount      | 3348.07          |
| DaysApart   | 1                |
# The amount and the one day are the whole of the case for these being one
# payment, which is why it is a question and not a decision.

Scenario Answering yes makes the two halves one transfer
Description The card's row keeps its own payee and gets the checking account as
\ its other side, and the row already in the book stops being an
\ expense -- which is what keeps 3,348.07 out of the spending.
