# pass's; see Payees. What is new here is only the set of transactions.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splitting a transaction that is already in the register
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A transaction already in the register is split from the same menu,
\ in the split window Transactions specifies.
Details A downloaded transaction arrives with one category, and the category it
\ arrives with is usually Uncategorized. One payment covering three
\ things is ordinary -- a trip to the hardware store, a cheque that was
\ two invoices -- and the only way to split one was to type it in as a
\ new entry, which a row that came from a file cannot be.
\
\ So it is the same window, opened on a transaction that is already
\ there rather than on a blank line, and what it saves is checked the
\ same way: the lines must come to the amount the transaction already
\ has. Nothing else about the transaction changes -- not its amount,
\ its date, its payee or its cleared status. A split says where the
\ money went, and the register's own side of it is already settled.
\
\ One transaction at a time, because the total the window checks
\ against is that transaction's amount. A split already made is shown
\ and not reopened: the lines of an imported split are what the file
\ said, and changing them is a separate question from being able to
\ make one at all.

Attributes SplitSaved
Description What came of saving the split window over a transaction that was
\ already in the register.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Saved     | Boolean | false   |
| Reason    | String  | none    |

Scenario Splitting an uncategorised transaction writes its lines
Description The payment is 184.50 against Uncategorized, which is what a
\ download leaves. Two lines are typed and they come to it, so the
\ transaction becomes a split and its category column says so.
