# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From a figure in a report to the transaction behind it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A report that shows its transactions should let one be opened. The
\ figure is the question; the register is where the answer is, and
\ where it can be corrected.
Details The register opened is the one of the real account the transaction
\ touches -- the bank or the card side. A category has no register to
\ browse: categories are reached through a report, which is where the
\ click started.
\
\ The line is selected, and the window scrolls it into view. Twenty years
\ of history is tens of thousands of lines, so a selection nobody can see
\ is the same as no selection at all.

Attributes ReportLineSelect
Description Which line of the report was clicked, counting from one.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Line      | Integer | 1       |

Define FebruaryOnChecking
Description The book the next three scenarios read. Two visits to a filling
\ station and one to a supermarket, so that one category figure has
\ more than one transaction behind it.
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-01 | Shell         | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | -40.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -60.00 |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-10 | Shell         | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | -30.00 |

# Waiting on the report itself living in the workspace rather than in the
# window, which is what a report tab needs; see the tabs section above.
Scenario Clicking a transaction in the report opens its register at that line
Description The fifth line of the report is the second of the two visits to the
\ filling station. It is the third line of the checking register, and
\ that is where the click lands.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
=FebruaryOnChecking
When report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category     |
| From     | 2026-02-01   |
| To       | 2026-02-28   |
| Interval | Whole        |
| Detail   | Transactions |
And report line clicked : ReportLineSelect Vertical
| Line | 5 |
Then open tabs are : OpenTab
| Position | Kind     | Account         | Name                 | Active |
| 1        | Report   | none            | Spending by category | false  |
| 2        | Register | Assets:Checking | Checking             | true   |
And panes are : PaneState
| Pane | Showing  | Account         | Active |
| 1    | Register | Assets:Checking | true   |
And the register line selected is : RegisterSelect
| Line |
| 3    |
# The report reads: Expenses, Expenses:Auto, Expenses:Auto:Fuel, the 1 February
# visit, the 10 February visit, Expenses:Groceries, the supermarket. So line
# five is the 10 February visit, and the checking register in date order has it
# third. Assets:Checking is the register opened because it is the real account
# the transaction touches; Expenses:Auto:Fuel has no register to browse.

