# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The width of a column
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Dragging the divider between two headings makes a column wider or
\ narrower. The width belongs to the column, is the same in every
\ register, and is kept with the book.
Details Every register is the same shape -- the same ten columns in the same
\ order, whatever the account -- so a width learned in one of them is
\ right in all of them. Widths held per account would mean widening the
\ payee column once per account, twenty times, for one preference about
\ how long a payee name is. Nobody would do that twice.
\
\ Kept with the book rather than with the program, for the same reason
\ every other setting is: a book is a file, and a book carried to
\ another machine should look the way it was left. See how a book is
\ stored in Books. The widths are one row per column there, keyed by the
\ column's name as this specification writes it -- not by its heading,
\ because Payment and Deposit are headed differently for different
\ account types and the width is not.
\
\ Only a column somebody resized is recorded. A column nobody touched
\ keeps whatever width the program gives it, and that number is not
\ stated anywhere here, so changing it does not fail a test. This is the
\ one thing that distinguishes "wide by default", which is appearance,
\ from "wide because I said so", which is not.

Attributes RegisterColumnWidth
Description The width of one register column, in pixels. A width is a number
\ only because a drag produces one; see the rule above for why no
\ default width appears in any table.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Column    | Text    |         |
| Width     | Integer | 0       |

BusinessRule A column cannot be dragged away to nothing
Description A width is clamped to a minimum. A column dragged to no width at
\ all would be invisible, and a column that cannot be seen cannot be
\ dragged back, so the program would have lost it until the book was
\ edited by hand.
Examples: WidthRequested
| Requested | Stored | Notes                                   |
| 240       | 240    | Taken as asked                          |
| 20        | 20     | The minimum exactly                     |
| 8         | 20     | Clamped; the divider stops here         |
| 0         | 20     | A drag past the next column stops here  |

Attributes WidthRequested
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Requested | Integer | 0       |
| Stored    | Integer | 0       |
| Notes     | String  | none    |

Scenario A column widened in one register is that wide in every register
Description The width is the column's, so moving to another account does not
\ undo it. Both of these registers have a payee column, and after
\ the drag there is one answer to how wide it is.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       | false  |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Payee |
| Width  | 240   |
And account selected : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column | Width |
| Payee  | 240   |

Scenario Only the columns somebody resized are recorded
Description The register has ten columns and one of them was dragged, so one
\ of them is written down. The other eight are whatever the program
\ makes them, which is deliberately not a number this
\ specification knows.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Memo |
| Width  | 180  |
Then recorded column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth
| Column | Width |
| Memo   | 180   |

Scenario A width survives the book being saved and opened again
Description A preference set once and lost on exit is worse than none, because
\ it has to be set again every time and it looks like a defect.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Payee |
| Width  | 240   |
And register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Category |
| Width  | 300      |
And the book is saved and opened again
And account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column   | Width |
| Payee    | 240   |
| Category | 300   |

Scenario A column dragged to nothing keeps the minimum width
Description The clamp from the rule above, through the window rather than in a
\ table of its own, because this is the case that would otherwise
\ lose a column for good.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | CheckNo |
| Width  | 0       |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column  | Width |
| CheckNo | 20    |

Attributes BookWrites
Description How many times the book was written. A count appears in a table
\ here for one reason only -- that writing it is the expensive thing
\ the program does, so how often is a fact about the program and not
\ about its speed on a particular machine.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Writes    | Integer | 0       |

Description A drag writes the width once, when the drag stops.
Details A drag is not one event. Every pixel of it is reported, so a divider
\ moved forty pixels is forty widths, and the register wrote the whole
\ book for each of them. On a book of thirty thousand transactions
\ that is about a second each, which is why widening the payee column
\ appeared to hang the program.
\
\ The widths that end up in the book are the same either way, and the
\ rule above about which columns are recorded is unchanged. What is
\ said here is when: the width is kept as the drag goes, so the
\ register redraws, and it is written when the drag has stopped.
\ Nothing is lost by waiting, because a width nobody has finished
\ choosing is not yet an answer.
\
\ What is written is the widths and nothing else. A width is not part of
\ the book in the way a transaction is -- it is where somebody left a
\ divider -- and the whole-book write rewrites every account,
\ transaction, split and rule to record one integer. That is both too
\ much work for a drag and a reason for a drag to fail over something
\ else in the book, which is how it was first seen.

Scenario Dragging a divider writes the width once, when the drag stops
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And register column dragged through : RegisterColumnWidth
| Column | Width |
| Payee  | 200   |
| Payee  | 220   |
| Payee  | 240   |
And the drag stops
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column | Width |
| Payee  | 240   |
And book writes are : BookWrites Vertical
| Writes | 1 |
# Three widths reported, the last of them kept, and one write. The two before
# it were on their way somewhere and were never an answer to anything.

Scenario A drag that is still going has written nothing yet
Description The register is already showing the width -- the column moves with
\ the divider -- and the book has not been touched. This is the half
\ of the rule that makes the one above worth having.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
And register column dragged through : RegisterColumnWidth
| Column | Width |
| Payee  | 200   |
| Payee  | 220   |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column | Width |
| Payee  | 220   |
And book writes are : BookWrites Vertical
| Writes | 0 |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The scroll bar
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes ScrollBarClick
| Attribute     | Type | Default |
| WhereClicked  | Text |         |
| WhatHappens   | Text |         |

BusinessRule Clicking the scroll bar beside the indicator pages the view
Description A register of thirty thousand lines is reached by its scroll bar
\ more than by anything else, and the click a person makes most is
\ the one in the empty part of the bar -- above the indicator to go
\ back a screen, below it to go on a screen. Dragging the indicator
\ in a register that long moves a hundred lines to the pixel, which
\ is no way to read a month.
Details A rule rather than a scenario because there is nothing in the model
\ to assert on. This specification describes a register as the lines
\ it holds and a report as the lines it holds; which of them is on
\ screen at this moment is not one of them. The behaviour is the
\ window's, and it is the same in a report as in a register.
\
\ A page is one screenful less a line, so the line that was at the top
\ before a page back is at the bottom after it. The overlap is what
\ keeps a reader's place: a page that moved a whole screenful would
\ leave nothing in common between the two views, and the eye would
\ have to start again.
Examples: ScrollBarClick
| WhereClicked           | WhatHappens                              |
| Above~the~indicator    | The~view~pages~back~one~screenful~less~a~line |
| Below~the~indicator    | The~view~pages~on~one~screenful~less~a~line |
| On~the~indicator       | Nothing~until~it~is~dragged              |
| On~an~arrow~at~the~end | The~view~moves~one~line                  |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setting up a new book
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A book is asked one question when it is made, and never again: is
\ there a Quicken history to bring in, or does this book start from
\ nothing? Both answers leave a book with a chart in it, because a
\ book with no accounts cannot be typed into and the first thing
\ anybody does with a new book is type into it.
Details Importing a history was an item on the File menu, next to Open, and
\ it had no business being there. It is the one import that can only
\ ever happen once, before anything else is in the book -- running it
\ over a book already in use would double twenty years of history --
\ and a menu that offers it every day is a menu that offers it on the
\ wrong day. Asked once, when the book is made, it cannot be asked at
\ the wrong time.
\
\ The history is a QIF. That is what Quicken exports and it is the only
\ format this question accepts: a QFX or a CSV is a statement for one
\ account, which is the ordinary import and belongs on the File menu
\ where it is. There is no second book to point at either -- a
\ SimpleAccount book is opened, not imported.
\
\ Declining the history is an answer rather than an escape. The book is
\ already made and named by the time the question is asked, so there is
\ nothing to back out of; the blank answer is the one that gives the
\ standard chart.

Attributes NewBookSetup
Description The question, as it stands. Shown is false before a book is made
\ and after the question is answered either way.
| Attribute   | Type    | Default |
| Shown       | Boolean | true    |
| BookName    | Text    | none    |
| HistoryFile | Text    | none    |

Attributes NewBookAnswer
Description What was answered. A blank HistoryFile is the answer "this is an
\ entirely new book"; it is not a cancellation.
| Attribute   | Type | Default |
| HistoryFile | Text | none    |

Scenario Naming a new book asks whether there is a history to bring in
Description The question arrives on its own, after the name and before
\ anything is in the book.
When a new book is made named : BookName Vertical
| Name | Household |
Then the new book question is : NewBookSetup Vertical
| Shown       | true      |
| BookName    | Household |
| HistoryFile | none      |

Scenario A book that starts from nothing starts with the standard chart
Description Three accounts somebody can type into on the first day, and a
\ category list taken from Quicken's own, because a stranger opening
\ this program expects the categories they have used for twenty
\ years. The real accounts are plain top-level names -- the account
\ list groups them by type already, so Assets would be a word on the
\ page saying what the heading beside it says. The categories are
\ under their fixed roots, because the rest of the program finds a
\ category by that literal path; see the account window in this file.
Details Uncategorized is in the list and is not Quicken's. It is this
\ program's answer to "what was the money for, nobody said", and an
\ import puts rows in it from the first file onwards, so a new book
\ that lacked it would create it on the first import instead.
\
\ Every parent is a placeholder. A placeholder has no register of its
\ own and its total is what is underneath it, which is what Auto or
\ Utilities is for. The leaves are the categories a transaction is
\ actually posted to.
When a new book is made named : BookName Vertical
| Name | Household |
And the new book question is answered : NewBookAnswer Vertical
| HistoryFile | none |
Then the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                           | Type       | Placeholder |
| Checking                       | Checking   | false       |
| Savings                        | Savings    | false       |
| Credit~Card                    | CreditCard | false       |
| Expenses                       | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Auto                  | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel             | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Insurance        | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Registration     | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Service          | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Bank~Charge           | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Charity               | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Childcare             | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Clothing              | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Dining                | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Education             | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Entertainment         | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Gifts~Given           | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries             | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Home~Repair           | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Household             | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Insurance             | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Interest~Expense      | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Medical               | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Medical:Dentist       | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Medical:Doctor        | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Medical:Medicine      | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Miscellaneous         | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Mortgage~Interest     | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Personal~Care         | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Pet~Care              | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Recreation            | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Subscriptions         | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes                 | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal         | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes:Property        | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes:Social~Security | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes:State           | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Telephone             | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Travel                | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized         | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Utilities             | Expense    | true        |
| Expenses:Utilities:Cable       | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Utilities:Electric    | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Utilities:Gas         | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Utilities:Water       | Expense    | false       |
| Income                         | Income     | true        |
| Income:Bonus                   | Income     | false       |
| Income:Dividend                | Income     | false       |
| Income:Gifts~Received          | Income     | false       |
| Income:Interest                | Income     | false       |
| Income:Other~Income            | Income     | false       |
| Income:Salary                  | Income     | false       |

Scenario A book that starts from a history starts with the history own chart
Description The standard list is not added to a history. Twenty years of
\ Quicken has its own categories, they are the ones the imported
\ transactions name, and a standard list beside them would be forty
\ empty categories in every picker for the rest of the book life. So
\ the answer to the question decides which chart the book gets -- not
\ whether a chart is added to.
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
And the file contains
"""
!Type:Cat
NGroceries
E
^
NSalary
I
^
!Type:Bank
D01/15/2026
T-45.00
PHarris Teeter
LGroceries
^
"""
When a new book is made named : BookName Vertical
| Name | Household |
And the new book question is answered : NewBookAnswer Vertical
| HistoryFile | household.qif |
Then the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets             | Checking | true        |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses           | Expense  | true        |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
| Income             | Income   | true        |
| Income:Salary      | Income   | false       |
# The three roots are placeholders the import makes to hold what the file
# names, each typed as what is under it; see ImportQif. The whole chart is
# listed because the point of the scenario is which categories a new book has.
# Expenses:Uncategorized is not here: nothing in this file is uncategorized,
# and the category this program adds for itself is added when something needs
# it. The checking account is the one the !Type:Bank section is read into; see
# ImportQif for how an account is named when the file does not name one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One item for every file a transaction can arrive in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description There is one way transactions arrive from outside, and it is one
\ menu item: Import transactions. What the file happens to be --- a
\ CSV from a bank that offers nothing else, a QFX from one that
\ does, a QIF from an old statement someone kept --- is a detail of
\ the file, settled by reading it, and not a question to put to
\ somebody who only wants last month in the register.
Details There used to be three items. Import history from Quicken has moved
\ to the question a new book is asked; see the section above. Import
\ QIF for review was the same operation as Import transactions with
\ one format spelled out in its name, and having both meant choosing
\ the right item before choosing the file --- two decisions where the
\ file answers one of them.
\
\ So the dialog offers CSV, QFX and QIF together, and what is read is
\ decided by what was chosen. The formats are already separate below
\ this point: see ImportCsv, ImportOfx and ImportQif, each of which
\ reads its own and none of which cares how it was reached.

Attributes FileMenuItem
Description One item of the File menu, in the order shown. Separators are not
\ named: they group what is here and change nothing.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Item      | Text    |         |
| Enabled   | Boolean | true    |

Scenario The File menu has one import on it
Description Open recent is a submenu and is named here as the item that opens
\ it. The two imports that used to sit between Open and this one are
\ gone: one to the new book question, one into this item.
When the file menu is shown
Then file menu items are : FileMenuItem
| Item                   | Enabled |
| New~book...            | true    |
| Open~book...           | true    |
| Open~recent            | true    |
| Import~transactions... | true    |
| Exit                   | true    |

Attributes ImportFileOffered
Description One file the import dialog lists, and when it was last written. The
\ dialog shows more than this; these are the two things the order and
\ the filtering are about.
| Attribute | Type | Default    |
| Name      | Text |            |
| Modified  | Date | 2024-01-01 |

Scenario The import dialog lists the three formats, newest first
Description Newest first because the file wanted is almost always the one just
\ downloaded. Alphabetical order puts it wherever its bank name
\ falls, which in a folder holding a year of downloads from four
\ banks is nowhere near the top.
Given the folder contains : ImportFileOffered
| Name              | Modified   |
| crown-2026-01.csv | 2026-02-02 |
| visa-2026-01.qfx  | 2026-02-05 |
| statement.qif     | 2026-01-20 |
When the import file dialog is shown
Then the files offered are : ImportFileOffered
| Name              | Modified   |
| visa-2026-01.qfx  | 2026-02-05 |
| crown-2026-01.csv | 2026-02-02 |
| statement.qif     | 2026-01-20 |

Scenario The import dialog leaves out a file of any other kind
Description A book, a spreadsheet and a PDF statement live in the same folder
\ as the downloads, and none of them is something this program reads.
Given the folder contains : ImportFileOffered
| Name              | Modified   |
| crown-2026-01.csv | 2026-02-02 |
| household.sadb    | 2026-02-06 |
| budget.xlsx       | 2026-02-04 |
| statement.pdf     | 2026-02-03 |
When the import file dialog is shown
Then the files offered are : ImportFileOffered
| Name              | Modified   |
| crown-2026-01.csv | 2026-02-02 |

BusinessRule The import dialog is the program own, not the one Windows provides
Description Newest first is the whole reason. The order a native file dialog
\ sorts in belongs to the shell, not to the program that opened it,
\ and there is no asking it for a different one.
Details The trade is a dialog that looks like this program rather than like
\ the rest of the machine, which is a real loss and a small one next to
\ scrolling a year of downloads to find yesterday. Stated as a rule
\ because there is nothing in the model to assert on: which dialog was
\ opened is not a fact about the book.
Examples: DialogChoice
| WhatIsWanted              | NativeDialog                  | OwnDialog     |
| Newest~first              | Not~ours~to~set               | Set~by~us     |
| CSV,~QFX~and~QIF~together | Yes                           | Yes           |
| Looks~like~Windows        | Yes                           | No            |

Attributes DialogChoice
| Attribute    | Type | Default |
| WhatIsWanted | Text |         |
| NativeDialog | Text |         |
| OwnDialog    | Text |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the disposition column reads
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes DispositionReading
| Attribute   | Type              | Default |
| Disposition | ImportDisposition | New     |
| Note        | DuplicateNote     | None    |
| Reads       | Text              |         |

BusinessRule The disposition column reads the disposition and then the note
Description One column, because the note is never anything but a qualification
\ of the disposition, and two columns would leave one of them empty on
\ every row. A plain duplicate reads Duplicate; a duplicate with
\ something to say says it after a dash.
Details The note is the whole of what the old Matched disposition used to
\ carry, and this is why losing it cost nothing: a row that reads
\ "Duplicate - identifier added" says what Matched said, in words,
\ beside the answer that actually matters. See the disposition list in
\ ImportOfx.
\
\ This is the reading of the row in front of somebody. The same note is
\ written on the transaction the duplicate found and stays there: the
\ row goes away when the import is accepted, and the question "why is
\ this cleared" does not. See a note an import left on a transaction.
\
\ A New or a Completed row has no note, because neither of them found
\ a transaction to disagree with. A Possible has none either --- what
\ is unresolved about a Possible is which row goes with which
\ transaction, and that is the disposition itself rather than a detail
\ of it.
\
\ Several notes can be true of one duplicate, as the check scenario in
\ ImportOfx shows. The row reads them in the order the note list
\ declares them, separated by commas after the one dash.
Examples: DispositionReading
| Disposition | Note            | Reads                             |
| New         | None            | New                               |
| Completed   | None            | Completed                         |
| Possible    | None            | Possible                          |
| Duplicate   | None            | Duplicate                         |
| Duplicate   | DetailsDiffer   | Duplicate~-~details~differ        |
| Duplicate   | CheckNumberSame | Duplicate~-~check~number~the~same |
| Duplicate   | FitIdAdded      | Duplicate~-~identifier~added      |
| Duplicate   | PayeeUnmatched  | Duplicate~-~payee~unmatched       |
| Duplicate   | Uncleared       | Duplicate~-~uncleared             |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Saying that something long is going on
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes ProgressReport
Description What the window is saying about work in hand. Finished is true once
\ the work is done, and the report goes away.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| What      | Text    | none    |
| Done      | Integer | 0       |
| Total     | Integer | 0       |
| Reads     | Text    | none    |
| Finished  | Boolean | false   |

Attributes ProgressAdvance
Description How far the work has got, so that a scenario can say so without a
\ real file and a real clock.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Done      | Integer | 0       |

BusinessRule Work that takes a noticeable time says so, and does not take the window away
Description An import of a twenty-year file and a recalculation of every
\ balance both take long enough that a still window reads as a hung
\ one. Each says what it is doing and how far it has got.
Details Modeless, for two reasons. The first is that a modal dialog over a
\ long import is a window that cannot be moved aside to look at what
\ is underneath, and the thing underneath is usually the register the
\ import is about to change. The second is that modal here would be a
\ lie: the work is not a question, nothing is being asked, and the
\ only thing the person can usefully do is stop it.
\
\ So it is a strip the window shows, not a dialog in front of it: what
\ is going on, how far it has got, and a way to stop. Everything that
\ was readable before stays readable. Nothing that would change the
\ book is offered while the work runs, because a book changing under
\ a recalculation is the one thing worse than a slow one.
\
\ Where there is a total it counts towards it, because a count with a
\ total is a time estimate and a count alone is not. A file import
\ knows its total: the rows were read before the first one was
\ reviewed. A recalculation knows the number of transactions, so it
\ has a total too. Something with no total counts what it has done
\ and says so in those words rather than showing a bar filling up to
\ a number nobody knows.
\
\ The threshold is a second. Work that finishes sooner never shows
\ anything, because a strip that appears and goes in the same blink
\ is worse than no strip -- the person sees a flicker and looks for
\ what it said. So the report is asked for at the start and shown
\ only if the work is still going a second later.
Examples: ProgressShown
| TheWork                      | HasATotal | WhatItSays                        |
| Reading a file of 40 rows    | Yes       | Nothing; it is over within a second |
| Importing 24,000 rows        | Yes       | Importing~--~8,000~of~24,000      |
| Recalculating every balance  | Yes       | Recalculating~--~8,000~of~24,000  |
| Opening a book               | No        | Opening~the~book                  |
| A report over twenty years   | Yes       | Building~the~report~--~3~of~20~years |

Attributes ProgressShown
| Attribute  | Type   | Default |
| TheWork    | String |         |
| HasATotal  | String |         |
| WhatItSays | String |         |

Scenario An import of a long file says what it is doing and how far it has got
Description The count is of rows, which is the unit the person chose the file
\ in, rather than of transactions or of postings or of bytes.
Given work in progress is : ProgressReport Vertical
| What  | Importing |
| Total | 24000     |
When work has advanced to : ProgressAdvance Vertical
| Done | 8000 |
Then work in progress is : ProgressReport Vertical
| What     | Importing              |
| Done     | 8000                   |
| Total    | 24000                  |
| Reads    | Importing~-~8,000~of~24,000 |
| Finished | false                  |

Scenario Work with no total counts what it has done
Description Opening a book does not know how much there is until it has read
\ it, so there is nothing to count towards and the strip says what it
\ is doing without pretending to know how long it will take.
Given work in progress is : ProgressReport Vertical
| What  | Opening~the~book |
| Total | 0                |
When work has advanced to : ProgressAdvance Vertical
| Done | 1200 |
Then work in progress is : ProgressReport Vertical
| What     | Opening~the~book |
| Done     | 1200             |
| Total    | 0                |
| Reads    | Opening~the~book |
| Finished | false            |
# No count in the words: a number with nothing to be a fraction of tells the
# reader nothing they can use.

Scenario Work that finishes takes its report away
Description The strip is not a log. When the work is done the window goes back
\ to what it was, and what the import did is said in the import's own
\ notes; see the notes shown when the import finishes in ImportOfx.
Given work in progress is : ProgressReport Vertical
| What  | Importing |
| Total | 24000     |
When work has advanced to : ProgressAdvance Vertical
| Done | 24000 |
Then work in progress is : ProgressReport
| What | Done | Total | Reads | Finished |
# No rows: nothing is in progress, so there is no report at all.

Scenario Stopping a recalculation leaves the book as it was
Description The only thing the strip offers is a way to stop, so what stopping
\ means has to be stated. A recalculation computes; it is the writing
\ at the end that changes anything, and a stopped one never gets
\ there.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -10.00  |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -20.00  |
When work in progress is stopped
Then transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -10.00  |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -20.00  |
And work in progress is : ProgressReport
| What | Done | Total | Reads | Finished |
# No rows. A stopped job is over, the same as a finished one, and the strip
# goes away either way.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tags
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes TagListingRow
Description One line of the tag listing: the tag, and how many transactions
\ carry it.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Name      | Text    |         |
| Used      | Integer | 0       |

Attributes TagEdit
Description A tag being renamed from the listing.
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| WasName   | Text | none    |
| Name      | Text | none    |

Attributes TagTyped
Description What has been typed into the tag column, what the program is
\ offering against it, and what the field holds when the typing is
\ accepted.
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Typed     | Text | none    |
| Offered   | Text | none    |
| Accepted  | Text | none    |

BusinessRule A tag is the third thing a transaction says about itself
Description A category says what the money was for and an account says where it
\ went. A tag says which part of the author's life it belongs to --
\ which property, which company -- and it cuts across both of the
\ others.
Details This is why it is not a category. Repairs on the rental and repairs
\ on the house are the same expense and want to be one line of a
\ spending report; which of the two it was is the tag. A category
\ tree that carried the distinction would have to carry it under
\ every category that could be either, and that is the Quicken class
\ mistake in reverse.
\
\ The field holds words, and every word in it is a tag. So a
\ transaction can carry several -- "Rental Oak~Street" is two tags and
\ not one tag with a space in it -- and a tag is therefore a single
\ word. This is the author's own statement of it, and it is why the
\ field stays one Text column on the transaction rather than becoming
\ a table between transactions and tags: the words are the list, and
\ splitting them is reading, not storage.
\
\ A tag with a space in it is therefore not available. Where one is
\ wanted the words are joined -- OakStreet, Oak_Street -- which is the
\ price of the field being a sentence rather than a reference. See the
\ tag table rule in Books.
\
\ A split's parts carry the transaction's tags. A split exists because
\ one payment covered several categories, not because it covered
\ several properties, and a per-part tag would be a second way of
\ saying what the category column already says.
\
\ No report groups by tag in this release; see the QIF migration note.
\ The tags are kept, listed and typed, and what a report does with
\ them comes after.
Examples: WhatATagIsFor
| Example                      | Category                  | TheField           | TagsItCarries |
| Paint for the rental         | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Rental             | 1             |
| Paint for the house          | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | none               | 0             |
| Paint for the rental kitchen | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Rental~Kitchen     | 2             |
| A client invoice paid        | Income:Consulting         | WarpAndByte        | 1             |
| Groceries                    | Expenses:Groceries        | none               | 0             |

Attributes WhatATagIsFor
| Attribute      | Type    | Default |
| Example        | String  |         |
| Category       | String  |         |
| TheField       | String  | none    |
| TagsItCarries  | Integer | 0       |

Scenario The tag listing is every tag with the number of transactions carrying it
Description The same window as the account listing and the category listing,
\ reached the same way, from the menu. A transaction whose field has
\ two words counts towards both of them.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee      | Destination               | Amount  | Tag            |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | -100.00 | Rental         |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | -50.00  | Rental~Kitchen |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-12 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | -25.00  | none           |
When tag listing opened
Then tag listing rows are : TagListingRow
| Name    | Used |
| Kitchen | 1    |
| Rental  | 2    |
# In name order. T3 carries no tag and so is no row: an untagged transaction is
# not a tag called nothing; see the two kinds of unknown in Transactions.

Scenario A tag nothing carries is still in the listing
Description The list is a vocabulary, so a tag is in it because it was used
\ once and not because something is using it at the moment. A year
\ with no rental transactions in it is not a reason to forget the
\ word.
Given tags are : TagListingRow
| Name        | Used |
| Rental      | 0    |
| WarpAndByte | 0    |
When tag listing opened
Then tag listing rows are : TagListingRow
| Name        | Used |
| Rental      | 0    |
| WarpAndByte | 0    |

Scenario Renaming a tag renames that word on every transaction carrying it
Description The tag is a word in a field, so a rename replaces the word and
\ leaves the other words in the field alone.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee      | Destination               | Amount  | Tag            |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | -100.00 | Rental         |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | -50.00  | Rental~Kitchen |
When tag renamed : TagEdit Vertical
| WasName | Rental    |
| Name    | OakStreet |
Then transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee      | Destination               | Amount  | Tag               |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | -100.00 | OakStreet         |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | -50.00  | OakStreet~Kitchen |
And tag listing rows are : TagListingRow
| Name      | Used |
| Kitchen   | 1    |
| OakStreet | 2    |

BusinessRule Typing a tag works the way typing a category works
Description The same completion, because a person typing into a register should
\ not have to remember which columns help and which do not.
Details The first character typed brings up the tags that match it, and one
\ is taken by clicking it or by the selection button, exactly as the
\ category column offers accounts and categories. The author asked
\ for it in those words: this is the same way entering the letters of
\ a category works.
\
\ Matching is on the word being typed and not on the whole field, so a
\ second tag can be completed after a first one has been accepted. The
\ word being typed is the one after the last space.
\
\ Matching ignores case, as the category column's does, and a tag that
\ is a prefix of another can still be typed in full: the offer is an
\ offer, and typing on past it replaces it.
\
\ A word that matches no tag is accepted and added to the table. This
\ is the one place a tag is created, which is why the listing needs no
\ New button for the common case: tagging a transaction with a word
\ nobody has used before is how a tag comes to exist.
\
\ Nothing is offered for an empty column, and nothing offers to
\ complete a tag to the empty string. Clearing the column takes every
\ tag off the transaction; it does not create a tag with no name.
Examples: TagCompletion
| Typed          | Offered     | Notes                                           |
| none           | none        | Nothing typed, nothing offered                  |
| R              | Rental      | The first character is enough to bring the list |
| r              | Rental      | Case is ignored, as in the category column      |
| Ren            | Rental      |                                                 |
| W              | WarpAndByte |                                                 |
| Z              | none        | No tag starts with Z; what is typed stands      |
| Rental~K       | Kitchen     | The word being typed is the one after the space |
| Rental~Kitchen | Kitchen     | Typing a word out in full is still a match      |

Attributes TagCompletion
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Typed     | String | none    |
| Offered   | String | none    |
| Notes     | String | none    |

Scenario A word nobody has used before is a new tag
Description The common way a tag comes to exist. It is on the transaction and
\ in the listing afterwards, and the next transaction can complete
\ it.
Given tags are : TagListingRow
| Name   | Used |
| Rental | 0    |
When tag typed is : TagTyped Vertical
| Typed    | OakStreet |
| Accepted | OakStreet |
Then tag listing rows are : TagListingRow
| Name      | Used |
| OakStreet | 0    |
| Rental    | 0    |
# Listed in name order, which is the order the listing is in. Used is 0 because
# this scenario is about the table and not about a transaction.

Scenario Clearing the tag column takes the tags off and creates nothing
Description A tag with no name is not a tag. The transaction goes back to
\ carrying none, and the listing is as it was.
Given tags are : TagListingRow
| Name   | Used |
| Rental | 0    |
When tag typed is : TagTyped Vertical
| Typed    | none |
| Accepted | none |
Then tag listing rows are : TagListingRow
| Name   | Used |
| Rental | 0    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Naming a real account in the destination column
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes DestinationTyped
Description The line being edited and the destination just put into it.
| Attribute   | Type           | Default |
| Ref         | TransactionRef |         |
| Destination | AccountPath    |         |

Attributes TransferCandidateRow
Description One transaction in the named account that could be the other half
\ of this one, read off the book. Closest in days first.
| Attribute | Type           | Default    |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |            |
| Account   | AccountPath    | none       |
| Date      | Date           | 2026-01-01 |
| Payee     | Text           | none       |
| Amount    | Money          | 0.00       |
| Cleared   | ClearedStatus  | Uncleared  |
| DaysApart | Integer        | 0          |

Attributes TransferCandidateChoice
Description Which candidate was chosen, or none. None is a real answer and not
\ an unanswered question; see the rule.
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Ref       | Text | none    |

BusinessRule Naming an account in the destination column looks for the other half
Description A category typed into the destination column is the end of the
\ matter. A real account is not: the account on the other side may
\ already have its own transaction for this payment, and now there are
\ two records of one thing.
Details This is the same question the import asks, asked from the register
\ instead, and it is asked for the same reason. Somebody who records
\ a card payment in the checking register and then downloads the card
\ has the payment twice; somebody who downloads both has it twice; and
\ somebody who types both has it twice. The moment to ask is the
\ moment the second account is named, because that is the first moment
\ there is anything to ask about.
\
\ What makes a candidate is what makes one for an import: equal and
\ opposite to the cent, in the account just named, within the transfer
\ window of two days, and not already paired with anything. The payee
\ is not consulted -- most payments have none recorded against the
\ other account -- and the candidates are offered closest in days
\ first. See the rule about a half already in the book in ImportOfx:
\ this is that rule, reached from the register.
\
\ Choosing a candidate leaves one transaction where there were two. A
\ transfer is one transaction naming both accounts, so the pairing is
\ a merge and not a link: the transaction being edited keeps its own
\ date, payee and memo, names the other account as its destination,
\ and the transaction that was in the other register is gone. Its
\ cleared status is not lost -- a half the bank has reported stays
\ reported -- so the merged transaction is cleared if either half was.
\
\ Answering none says the other account has no record of this yet.
\ That is the ordinary case and it creates nothing: the transaction
\ already names both accounts, so it is already a line in the other
\ register, and that line is uncleared because no bank has reported
\ it. What none rules out is the merge, not the transfer.
\
\ A destination that is a category, a destination that is Unassigned,
\ and a destination that is a real account with no candidate in it are
\ all silent. The question is only asked when there is something to
\ choose between, because a dialog that says "there is nothing to ask
\ you" is worse than no dialog.
Examples: DestinationNamed
| WhatIsTyped                              | WhatHappens                                   |
| A category                               | Nothing; the category is the whole answer     |
| Unassigned                               | Nothing; the account is not known yet         |
| An account with no equal and opposite transaction | Nothing; it is a transfer and that is all |
| An account with one                      | The candidate is offered                      |
| An account with three                    | All three are offered, closest in days first  |

Attributes DestinationNamed
| Attribute   | Type   | Default |
| WhatIsTyped | String |         |
| WhatHappens | String |         |

Scenario A destination changed to an account offers the matching transaction
Description The card payment entered in the checking register, and the card
\ register already holding the other side of it. Naming the card is
\ what finds it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | false     |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee        | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-02-10 | Visa~Payment | Expenses:Uncategorized | -3348.07 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-12 | Payment      | Expenses:Uncategorized | 3348.07  | Cleared   |
When destination typed is : DestinationTyped Vertical
| Ref         | T1               |
| Destination | Liabilities:Visa |
Then transfer candidates are : TransferCandidateRow
| Ref | Account          | Date       | Payee   | Amount  | Cleared | DaysApart |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-12 | Payment | 3348.07 | Cleared | 2         |

Scenario Choosing the match leaves one transfer where there were two transactions
Description The merge. One transaction afterwards, naming both accounts, and it
\ is cleared because the card half was.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | false     |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee        | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-02-10 | Visa~Payment | Expenses:Uncategorized | -3348.07 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-12 | Payment      | Expenses:Uncategorized | 3348.07  | Cleared   |
When destination typed is : DestinationTyped Vertical
| Ref         | T1               |
| Destination | Liabilities:Visa |
And transfer candidate chosen : TransferCandidateChoice Vertical
| Ref | T2 |
Then transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee        | Destination      | Amount   | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-02-10 | Visa~Payment | Liabilities:Visa | -3348.07 | Cleared |
# T2 is gone: it was the other half, and a transfer is one transaction. T1 keeps
# its own date and payee, and keeps the cleared status the card half brought.

Scenario Choosing none leaves an uncleared line in the other register
Description The ordinary case. The card has not reported the payment yet, so
\ nothing in that register is the other half, and the transfer itself
\ is what puts a line there.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | false     |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee        | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-02-10 | Visa~Payment | Expenses:Uncategorized | -3348.07 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-12 | Payment      | Expenses:Uncategorized | 3348.07  | Cleared   |
When destination typed is : DestinationTyped Vertical
| Ref         | T1               |
| Destination | Liabilities:Visa |
And transfer candidate chosen : TransferCandidateChoice Vertical
| Ref | none |
Then transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee        | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-02-10 | Visa~Payment | Liabilities:Visa       | -3348.07 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-12 | Payment      | Expenses:Uncategorized | 3348.07  | Cleared   |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Date       | Payee        | Category    | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-02-10 | Visa~Payment | [Checking]  | Uncleared |
| T2  | 2026-02-12 | Payment      | Expenses:Uncategorized | Cleared |
# The transfer is a line in the card register without anything having been
# created, because the one transaction names both accounts. T2 is left alone:
# saying none says these two are not the same payment.

Scenario A category in the destination column asks nothing
Description Stated so that the question cannot start appearing on ordinary
\ typing. Most of what goes in that column is a category and none of
\ it is a transfer.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | false     |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee        | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-02-10 | Harris~Teeter | Expenses:Uncategorized | -3348.07 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-12 | Payment      | Expenses:Uncategorized | 3348.07  | Cleared   |
When destination typed is : DestinationTyped Vertical
| Ref         | T1                 |
| Destination | Expenses:Groceries |
Then transfer candidates are : TransferCandidateRow
| Ref | Account | Date | Payee | Amount | Cleared | DaysApart |
# No rows: a category is the whole answer, even though an equal and opposite
# transaction happens to exist in another account.

Scenario An account with nothing equal and opposite in it asks nothing
Description A transfer to an account that has no record of it is the common
\ case, and it is silent. Nothing is offered and nothing is asked.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings         | Savings  | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee    | Destination            | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-10 | Transfer | Expenses:Uncategorized | -500.00 | Uncleared |
When destination typed is : DestinationTyped Vertical
| Ref         | T1             |
| Destination | Assets:Savings |
Then transfer candidates are : TransferCandidateRow
| Ref | Account | Date | Payee | Amount | Cleared | DaysApart |
# No rows.
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee    | Destination    | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-02-10 | Transfer | Assets:Savings | -500.00 | Uncleared |

Scenario Three candidates are offered closest in days first
Description Three equal and opposite transactions in the named account inside
\ the window. The order is the order the question asks them in, and
\ the nearest date is the likeliest pairing.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | false     |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
And transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee        | Destination            | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking  | 2026-02-10 | Visa~Payment | Expenses:Uncategorized | -100.00 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-12 | Payment~A    | Expenses:Uncategorized | 100.00  | Cleared   |
| T3  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-11 | Payment~B    | Expenses:Uncategorized | 100.00  | Cleared   |
| T4  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-10 | Payment~C    | Expenses:Uncategorized | 100.00  | Uncleared |
When destination typed is : DestinationTyped Vertical
| Ref         | T1               |
| Destination | Liabilities:Visa |
Then transfer candidates are : TransferCandidateRow
| Ref | Account          | Date       | Payee     | Amount | Cleared   | DaysApart |
| T4  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-10 | Payment~C | 100.00 | Uncleared | 0         |
| T3  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-11 | Payment~B | 100.00 | Cleared   | 1         |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2026-02-12 | Payment~A | 100.00 | Cleared   | 2         |
